        <div id="sugar-sweetener" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. How Much Sugar and Sweetener Is in Marshmallow Protein Powder?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Marshmallow protein powders run from 0 g to 3 g of sugar per scoop, per the labels at the time of writing, and four of the five Amazon picks are sweetened with sucralose. That is far less sugar than real marshmallows, which is the whole appeal, but it is not nothing in the cereal-style tubs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Axe & Sledge Marshmallow Milk lists 0 g of sugar, Pure Choice lists 0.4 g and RYSE lists 1 g. GHOST Lucky Charms and LGXNDS Charmies each list 3 g, of which 2 g is added sugar from the marshmallow pieces. For most people 2 g is trivial, about half a teaspoon. For anyone tracking added sugar closely, including people managing diabetes with a clinician's guidance, the flavoring-only powders are the cleaner choice.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sweeteners are where the five are most alike. GHOST, LGXNDS, Axe & Sledge and Pure Choice all list sucralose, and RYSE pairs sucralose with acesulfame potassium. LGXNDS markets its isolate as having zero artificial sweeteners, yet the Charmies panel lists sucralose, so read the panel rather than the product copy. If you want to avoid both, Clean Simple Eats Toasted Marshmallow uses stevia only, and our <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-artificial-sweeteners/">guide to protein powder without artificial sweeteners</a> lists more options.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sodium varies more than people expect. RYSE lists 240 mg per scoop and GHOST 190 mg, while Axe & Sledge lists 115 mg and Pure Choice only 25 mg. Two shakes a day of RYSE adds nearly 500 mg, about a fifth of the 2,300 mg daily limit in the US Dietary Guidelines, which matters if you are watching blood pressure.
          </p>

          <p>
            Lactose is the last label question. The isolates (Axe & Sledge, Pure Choice, LGXNDS) carry the least, and GHOST adds lactase enzyme to its blend. RYSE uses isolate plus concentrate and adds sodium caseinate through its MCT powder, so it is the least suited to lactose-sensitive drinkers. See our <a href="/best-lactose-free-protein-powder/">lactose-free protein powder guide</a> if that applies to you.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="taste" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. What Do Buyers Say About Marshmallow Protein Powder Taste?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buyers mostly describe RYSE Jet-Puffed Marshmallow as sweet and close to the bagged marshmallow, with a vanilla-forward finish, and it is one of the RYSE flavors that comes up most often in buyer praise. We have not tasted any of these powders; what follows is a summary of buyer reviews and feedback on retailer pages.
          </p>

          <p>
            The most common complaint about RYSE marshmallow is sweetness. Buyers who like it compare it to a melted marshmallow or a vanilla milkshake; those who do not say it is too sweet with water and better with milk or coffee. Buyers also describe the texture as creamy, which fits the MCT powder and gum blend on the label. If you want to see how it stacks up against GHOST on taste and price overall, our <a href="/ryse-vs-ghost-protein/">RYSE vs GHOST comparison</a> and the programmatic <a href="/compare/ghost-whey-vs-ryse-loaded-protein/">Ghost Whey vs RYSE Loaded label comparison</a> cover it.
          </p>

          <p>
            GHOST Lucky Charms buyers mostly react to the marshmallow pieces. Fans like finding them in the shake or in oats, and say the base tastes like sweet cereal milk. The complaints are that the pieces dissolve quickly in a shaker and that some scoops get fewer pieces than others. Buyers of GHOST's earlier Cereal Milk flavor often describe it as one of the brand's best, and the Lucky Charms version builds on that base.
          </p>

          <p>
            Feedback on the smaller brands is thinner. Axe & Sledge reviewers describe Marshmallow Milk as a light, sweet marshmallow cereal milk taste, and the brand has a loyal following for its dessert flavors. Independent feedback on LGXNDS Charmies and Pure Choice Frosted Marshmallow is scarce beyond seller-hosted reviews, so treat both as more of a gamble on flavor. Pure Choice's own store calls Frosted Marshmallow its most unusual designer flavor and suggests blending it with others.
          </p>

          <p>
            On mixing, the isolates are the thinnest and mix fastest. RYSE and GHOST mix thicker because of their concentrate and gums. All five are whey, so a shaker bottle is enough; a blender only helps if you are adding ice or frozen fruit. Cold liquid helps marshmallow flavors more than most, since buyers find they taste sweeter and flatter when warm.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="recipes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. What Can You Make With Marshmallow Protein Powder?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Marshmallow protein powder works best in cold, no-bake foods such as overnight oats, Greek yogurt bowls, cereal milk and blended shakes, and it can be used in hot cocoa if you whisk it in once the drink has cooled slightly. Its sweet vanilla-like profile pairs with chocolate, peanut butter, strawberry and coffee.
          </p>

          <p>
            The easiest upgrade is cereal milk. Shake one scoop of RYSE or Axe & Sledge with 8 to 10 oz of cold milk and pour it over cereal, or stir a scoop of GHOST Lucky Charms into a bowl of plain puffed cereal for the marshmallow pieces. A scoop stirred into 3/4 cup of Greek yogurt makes a thick marshmallow-fluff style dip for strawberries or apples, and adds another 15 g or so of protein from the yogurt.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a protein hot cocoa, make the cocoa first, let it drop below a simmer, then whisk in half a scoop at a time. Whey clumps in liquid that is too hot, so add it gradually. For a thicker dessert texture, some people mix marshmallow whey with a scoop of casein, which sets up like pudding in the fridge; our <a href="/best-micellar-casein-protein-powder/">micellar casein protein powder guide</a> covers which casein to pick.
          </p>

          <p>
            Baking is where marshmallow powders struggle. Whey dries out in long, hot bakes, and the marshmallow pieces in GHOST and LGXNDS melt and can scorch. Use them in no-bake bars, protein crispy treats made with puffed rice and nut butter, or mug cakes that cook in under two minutes. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-baking/">protein powder for baking guide</a> explains which powders handle heat better.
          </p>

          <p>
            If marshmallow is one of several sweet flavors you want to try, the same recipes work with birthday cake and fruity cereal powders. See our guides to <a href="/best-birthday-cake-protein-powder/">birthday cake protein powder</a> and <a href="/fruity-pebbles-protein-powder/">Fruity Pebbles protein powder</a>, and the full <a href="/best-tasting-protein-powder/">best-tasting protein powder rankings</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

